	What is the hardest input you can throw at it, and does it still hold up?
A panicked homeowner lying on their back under a kitchen sink, holding an iPhone in one hand and a wrench in the other. The input feed is chaotic: the front-facing camera is smeared with water, the only lighting is the phone’s flashlight reflecting harshly off chrome pipes, and the audio is a mix of spraying water and a barking dog.
It holds up because the system degrades gracefully. If the Decisions API detects extreme visual noise (glare, water drops, motion blur), the PWA stops streaming continuous video and uses the browser's MediaDevices API to snap three high-exposure, high-contrast burst photos. It shifts from video reasoning to image reasoning, prompting the user via voice: "I can't see the threading. Wipe the lens and hold still for two seconds."
What does your project get from the raw video, audio, or images that a text description would lose?
Absolute spatial and diagnostic reality. A user typing, "My dishwasher is making a weird grinding noise and the bottom thing is stuck," is a diagnostic dead end.
By ingesting the raw media directly from the user's phone, Gemini 4 hears the specific rhythmic cavitation of a failing impeller pump. It sees that the user's hand is trying to force a retaining clip counter-clockwise when the visual wear-patterns on the plastic clearly indicate a clockwise snap-lock. Text forces the user to translate a 3D physical problem into 1D words they often don't have the vocabulary for; raw media lets the AI diagnose the physics directly.
How far did you push before it broke, and what did you change to get past that point?
Break 1: Mobile browser thermal throttling and latency. Initially, we tried pumping a continuous 1080p WebRTC stream into gemini-4-carbon while the user was building a 120-piece patio set. By minute 20, the iPhone Safari browser was consuming so much memory and generating so much heat that the OS dimmed the screen to 30%, making the AR overlays invisible.The Fix: We stopped streaming continuous video to the heavy model. We built a local WebAssembly (Wasm) motion-detection script in the PWA. It only sends frames to the Decisions API when it detects the user's hands have stopped moving for more than 3 seconds (indicating confusion or completion of a step). We reduced API calls by 85% and eliminated thermal throttling entirely.
Break 2: The "Scale" Hallucination. When a user pointed an iPad at a stripped screw on a blank white wall, Gemini 4 couldn't determine the scale. It confidently generated a gemini-omni-1.2-flash video instructing the user to use a massive pipe wrench on what was actually a tiny 2mm electronics screw.The Fix: WebXR Depth Mapping and Anchor Objects. We updated the PWA to require a "scale anchor." Before diagnosing an isolated part, the app asks the user to place a standard object (a coin, a credit card, or their thumb) next to the broken item. The graph now enforces a strict rule: if depth data (via WebXR) or a known physical anchor is absent, gemini-4-neon is barred from suggesting a specific tool size, preventing catastrophic mechanical advice.